Uh, and quite often when we're drawing

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:02.190
timing diagrams or trying to explain

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.590
timing, um, we would show the fragment

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.510
of the algorithmic state machine next to

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:07.390
it.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.990
Uh, and and this allows direct

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.950
verification of the actual response,

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.550
that is the waveforms generated by the

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:14.070
circuit, against the behavior in the

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.310
chart. So, it can So, that's basically

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.070
how we would use these timing diagrams

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:19.350
as well as well as

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.710
ASM charts when analyzing the timing.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.790
So, and if if a finite So, here we've

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.350
got if a if a finite state machine is

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.350
used to control another circuit, then

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.030
all the state outputs should be enabling

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.510
circuits,

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:35.670
not clocking signals.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:37.870
So, that's that's kind of important, so

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.350
that the controlled output can use the

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.870
same clock. So, key thing in our design,

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:43.030
and particularly when we're designing

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.870
using FPGAs, but in fact any sort of

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.870
synchronous design, is we really want

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:48.670
everything to run on essentially the

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.110
same clock.

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:51.910
Um, it is possible to have multiple

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.550
clock domains, that's beyond the scope

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.190
of this course, but generally we want

00:14:55.200 --> 00:14:57.470
things on the same clock.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:58.870
Um, if you're not doing that, you have

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.470
to add in extra circuitry to synchronize

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:02.750
signals,

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:04.670
um, or perhaps add in buffers and things

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:05.990
like that.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.390
Um, so to do that, we need that the

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.950
outputs are enabling circuits. That is,

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.310
they are outputs which are then read

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.190
and they control the next state machine

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.150
on the clock edge. They're not outputs

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.430
which change in the immediate cause an

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:21.190
immediate change, let's say, in a state.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.350
So, immediate and enable outputs are

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:24.790
asserted by the finite state machine

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.590
just after the occurrence of the clock,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.590
so so as I mentioned before.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.550
So, state outputs are um

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.150
uh are generated just after entering

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:36.550
state, and they affect a change on the

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:38.710
control circuit on the next clock edge.

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:40.830
So, bear that in mind.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:42.670
If if a if one circuit one synchronous

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:44.510
state machine changes,

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.430
the outputs change just after

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:49.430
that will impact the next state machine

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.990
one clock edge later. So, if one cycle

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.270
later and so on.

